    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Insight into complex geological processes has often been gained through experimentation with simple physical models (Daubree 1879, Hubbert 1937, Ramberg 1967). However,this type of modeling can have limitations, as the constitutive properties of the model materials might be inappropriate or the boundary conditions might be difficult to impose correctly. With the advent of powerful computers, numerical experimentation can be used to bypass these obstacles, perhaps creating more realistic models. One such numerical technique, the boundary element method(BEM), has been used to model fault behavior (Mavko 1982, Bilham and King 1989), the emplacement of igneous dikes (Delaney and Pollard 1981), and the growth of joint sets (Olson and Pollard 1989). Here the BEMis used to analyze joint patterns in layered rock.\r\n\r\nThere have been a number of explanations of how joint spacing comes to be and what controls its development. The model discussed in this paper addresses the issue of quasi-static joint propagation in layered rock, some of its controlling influences, and the evolution of joint distributions through time.\r\n\r\nSpecifically, this model traces the evolution of the spacing between joints, their areal density, and the joint apertures as a function of the strain history of the layer. In practical terms, outcrop data for these parameters could potentially be utilized to back-out an area’s strain history and then reapply that history to create a more accurate fracture model for subsurface fluid flow.",
